          "type": "turn",
          "question": "A claim came back asking for more specificity on a three-character code. Is that always a reasonable request?",
          "answer": "**No, it isn't always reasonable.** It depends on whether the code can take more characters.\n\nICD-10-CM codes are three to seven characters long. The three-character form is the category, and characters four to six add detail such as cause, site, severity, or laterality (*ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*). Most categories need those extra characters before the code is complete. Some do not. Essential hypertension, I10, is complete at three characters, so a request to \"add more specificity to I10\" is one the classification cannot satisfy (*Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*).\n\nSo a request for more detail is reasonable when the category has further subdivisions that the record could support. It is not reasonable when the three-character code is already complete.\n\nI can't tell which code your claim used, and the retrieved sources don't say how any particular payer handles these requests. Two questions to take to the billing or coding team:\n\n- Does the code I was given have sub-codes, and does the clinical record support one of them?\n- If it is already complete, what exactly is the claim asking for?\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
